WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:04.569 --> 00:00:07.142
Imagine you are an investigation officer and

00:00:07.142 --> 00:00:09.539
you need to find the criminal in the case

00:00:09.539 --> 00:00:11.660
assigned to you.

00:00:11.660 --> 00:00:13.660
You are given samples of blood obtained from

00:00:13.660 --> 00:00:16.240
the crime scene and have three suspects in

00:00:16.250 --> 00:00:17.750
the list.

00:00:17.750 --> 00:00:22.140
How will you find out which suspect is the criminal?

00:00:22.140 --> 00:00:24.220
And the case doesn’t end here!

00:00:24.220 --> 00:00:26.920
Simultaneously, you have to hand over the

00:00:26.921 --> 00:00:28.970
little kid of the victim to one of the two

00:00:28.970 --> 00:00:32.340
men claiming to be the child’s father.

00:00:32.340 --> 00:00:35.500
How will you solve both the mysteries together?

00:00:36.960 --> 00:00:39.720
There is one solution for both the problems

00:00:39.725 --> 00:00:43.360
and it’s called “DNA fingerprinting”.

00:00:43.360 --> 00:00:45.860
This technique is very crucial in terms of

00:00:45.860 --> 00:00:49.440
advancement in modern science and technology.

00:00:49.450 --> 00:00:51.906
It is a technique that helps us analyse our

00:00:51.906 --> 00:00:54.420
unique DNA just like the unique fingerprints

00:00:54.420 --> 00:00:55.570
that we have!

00:00:55.570 --> 00:00:58.840
The profiles at the end help us analyse the

00:00:58.840 --> 00:01:01.700
DNA of every individual.

00:01:01.700 --> 00:01:04.460
For instance, looking at the results of the

00:01:04.460 --> 00:01:07.460
first part, we can be almost be sure that

00:01:07.470 --> 00:01:09.640
suspect 2 is the criminal.

00:01:09.640 --> 00:01:12.750
And similarly, in the second part, we can

00:01:12.750 --> 00:01:16.240
say that individual 1 is the biological father

00:01:16.240 --> 00:01:17.260
of the child.

00:01:18.400 --> 00:01:22.780
Does every person really have a unique DNA?

00:01:22.780 --> 00:01:24.040
Of course yes!

00:01:24.040 --> 00:01:27.940
DNA is termed as the “Blueprint of life”.

00:01:27.940 --> 00:01:30.113
It is a specific set of instructions that

00:01:30.113 --> 00:01:32.340
gives rise to the characters that are seen

00:01:32.340 --> 00:01:34.280
in the organisms.

00:01:34.280 --> 00:01:36.760
But this is something that we are aware of

00:01:36.760 --> 00:01:39.020
today and it wasn’t known back then!

00:01:39.660 --> 00:01:42.900
So who discovered that DNA is so important?

00:01:45.060 --> 00:01:48.140
Which scientist or scientists contributed

00:01:48.143 --> 00:01:51.219
towards finding out that DNA is the code for

00:01:51.220 --> 00:01:53.329
all the characteristics in us?

00:01:53.720 --> 00:01:56.840
Let us find that out by going back in time!

00:01:56.840 --> 00:02:00.080
Firstly, let us have a look at the milestones

00:02:00.080 --> 00:02:01.540
in this journey.

00:02:01.950 --> 00:02:05.160
To begin with, we have the father of genetics,

00:02:05.169 --> 00:02:08.039
Gregor Mendel, who introduced the concept

00:02:08.039 --> 00:02:10.039
of Heredity to us!

00:02:10.039 --> 00:02:13.016
He was the first to hypothesize that there

00:02:13.016 --> 00:02:15.639
are “FACTORS” which pass from parents

00:02:15.639 --> 00:02:17.889
to offsprings.

00:02:17.889 --> 00:02:20.180
This was around mid 1860s.

00:02:20.860 --> 00:02:24.639
However, his work was largely ignored.

00:02:24.639 --> 00:02:27.144
It was around the first decade of the 20th

00:02:27.144 --> 00:02:29.769
century that the two scientists came up with

00:02:29.769 --> 00:02:34.010
the theory which helped prove Mendelian genetics.

00:02:34.010 --> 00:02:35.660
Who were the two scientists?

00:02:35.660 --> 00:02:40.540
They were Walter Sutton and Theodor Boveri.

00:02:40.540 --> 00:02:43.436
They put forth the chromosomal theory of inheritance

00:02:43.436 --> 00:02:45.720
which helped shine some light on the long

00:02:45.720 --> 00:02:48.700
ignored work of Gregor Mendel.

00:02:48.700 --> 00:02:51.614
However, Sutton and Boveri’s work gained

00:02:51.614 --> 00:02:54.600
better acceptance with the next milestone

00:02:54.600 --> 00:02:56.900
which was achieved by the father of experimental

00:02:56.900 --> 00:02:59.780
genetics: Thomas Morgan.

00:02:59.780 --> 00:03:02.610
Morgan’s work on the little fruit fly was

00:03:02.610 --> 00:03:05.510
very instrumental in proving the fact that

00:03:05.510 --> 00:03:09.100
“genes are located on the chromosomes”.

00:03:09.100 --> 00:03:11.700
His experiments helped explain the location

00:03:11.700 --> 00:03:14.239
of eye colour genes on the “X” chromosomes

00:03:14.239 --> 00:03:17.570
in Drosophila melanogaster.

00:03:17.570 --> 00:03:20.590
We have seen the history of genetics until this time!

00:03:20.590 --> 00:03:25.479
Now it’s time we move a step ahead in the timeline!

00:03:25.479 --> 00:03:27.974
Until this time, scientists believed that

00:03:27.974 --> 00:03:30.591
genes, which are the units of heredity, are

00:03:30.591 --> 00:03:33.310
majorly made up of proteins.

00:03:33.310 --> 00:03:35.660
In other words, the chemical nature of the

00:03:35.660 --> 00:03:39.199
genetic material was thought to be Proteins.

00:03:39.199 --> 00:03:43.340
However, this wasn’t really considered to be a fact.

00:03:43.340 --> 00:03:44.870
It was just an ASSUMPTION.

00:03:45.400 --> 00:03:47.400
People were still trying to figure out what

00:03:47.404 --> 00:03:49.880
exactly could the genetic material be made

00:03:49.880 --> 00:03:50.940
up of!

00:03:51.400 --> 00:03:54.480
It was around 1928 that a breakthrough was

00:03:54.480 --> 00:03:56.400
achieved in this field.

00:03:57.360 --> 00:04:00.580
A British bacteriologist named Fredrick Griffith

00:04:00.584 --> 00:04:03.740
reported for the first time that tiny organisms

00:04:03.740 --> 00:04:07.047
like the bacteria are also capable of transferring

00:04:07.047 --> 00:04:08.360
genetic information.

00:04:08.920 --> 00:04:11.091
His famous “Transformation” experiment

00:04:11.100 --> 00:04:14.600
ignited the curiosity regarding genetic material.

00:04:15.360 --> 00:04:19.358
A few years later, around 1944, three scientists

00:04:19.358 --> 00:04:23.720
named Avery, MacLeod and McCarty figured out

00:04:23.720 --> 00:04:26.331
that the transformation principle, or the

00:04:26.331 --> 00:04:29.070
unit of heredity to be precise, is actually

00:04:29.070 --> 00:04:30.520
DNA.

00:04:31.040 --> 00:04:33.800
And this was strongly supported by the work

00:04:33.804 --> 00:04:36.240
of Hershey and Chase later around the year

00:04:36.240 --> 00:04:37.240
1952.

00:04:38.260 --> 00:04:40.975
The simple experiment by these two helped

00:04:40.975 --> 00:04:44.710
prove that it is DNA and not the proteins

00:04:44.710 --> 00:04:48.030
that form the core of the genetic material.

00:04:48.030 --> 00:04:51.242
Later, the structure of DNA, built by the

00:04:51.242 --> 00:04:54.690
efforts of Franklin, Watson and Crick formed

00:04:54.690 --> 00:04:58.840
a strong foundation for modern day genetics.

00:04:58.840 --> 00:05:01.560
This timeline helps us with the milestones

00:05:01.560 --> 00:05:03.700
achieved so far in genetics.

00:05:04.600 --> 00:05:06.880
Let us have a detailed look at each of these

00:05:06.884 --> 00:05:10.130
experiments and events in the upcoming parts!

